        "heading": "1 INTRODUCTION",
        "text": "Learning expressive representations from limited supervision is an enduring research topic. In computer vision, many well-established paradigms have been developed to create supervision without labels, which basically fall into two categories (Chen et al., 2020a): generative and discriminative. The discriminative ones, represented by contrastive learning (Chen et al., 2020a; He et al., 2020; Grill et al., 2020; Chen & He, 2021; Caron et al., 2020; Zbontar et al., 2021), used to lie at the center of visual self-supervised learning due to their well-proven representation properties (e.g., good linear separability) and compelling downstream performance. It is the introduction of masked modeling (Devlin et al., 2018; Clark et al., 2020) together with transformer architecture (Vaswani et al., 2017) from natural language processing (NLP) rekindled public interest in generative visual pre-training. These approaches (Bao et al., 2021; He et al., 2021; Xie et al., 2021; Fang et al., 2022) outperform their contrastive and supervised counterparts on vision transformers (Dosovitskiy et al., 2020; Touvron et al., 2021; Liu et al., 2021), thereby showing great promise. Curiously, the success has not yet spread to convolutional networks, especially since some advanced contrastive methods still remain state of the art (Caron et al., 2020; Grill et al., 2020; Tian et al., 2020; Chen et al., 2021). We therefore ask: what makes it difficult to apply masked modeling to convnets?\nIn essence, the difficulty is rooted in the gap between NLP and computer vision in terms of data processing (He et al., 2021; Devlin et al., 2018; Yang et al., 2019): transformer processes text as a variable-length sequence of words (well-defined semantic units), while convnet has to extract semantics from objects of different sizes (like \u201cunits\u201d at different scales). This big difference gives rise to the following challenges: (i) Removing information of masked part is difficult for convnet. In vision transformer, an input image is divided to non-overlapping patches. Simply dropping masked patches or replacing them with mask tokens removes the information. This ease relies on the transformer being able to handle irregular (variable-length) and non-overlapping patches, thus cannot be achieved on convnets as they not only operate on regular grids, but also perform sliding window with overlapping. One may zero-out all masked pixels and feed the image into convnet. This, however, leads to a severe data distribution shift (shown in figure 1) and cannot be an ideal solution.\n(ii) Single-scale algorithm cannot learn multi-scale convolutional features. Existing masked modeling typically works in a single-scale manner as the decoding process is based only on features at the end of encoder. In contrast, using multi-scale structure is acknowledged as a gold-standard in computer vision. It allows visual processing systems like SIFT/SURF descriptors (Lowe, 1999; Bay et al., 2006) as well as pyramid networks (He et al., 2015; Lin et al., 2017; Liu et al., 2016) to cope with changes in object scale. Applying vanilla masked modeling will miss the advantage of hierarchy.\nIn this paper, we develop a methodology called Sparse masKed modeling with hierarchy (SparK) to clear all the hurdles above. We first mask images in a patch-wise manner. Observing the sparse nature of point clouds coincides with those masked patches, we then sparsely gather all unmasked patches into a sparse image, and use sparse convolutions for encoding. This strategy accurately eliminates the information from masked part whilst allowing convnets to easily handle irregular masked images. For decoding, we fill in empty positions on the multi-scale encoded features with mask embeddings, and feed them into a multi-scale decoder. This way, we can embrace the advantage of feature hierarchy. Taken together, SparK makes masked modeling now well suited for any convolutional architectures.\nEvaluation is performed on standard downstream tasks including image classification, object detection, and instance segmentation. We observe that both classical (He et al., 2016) and modern (Liu et al., 2022b) convnets benefit a lot (by around +1.0%) from SparK, and with more performance gains on larger models that show the scalability. On all downstream tasks, convnet-based SparK outperforms both (i) state-of-the-art contrastive learning and (ii) transformer-based masked pre-training by similarly large margins (around +1.0%), which shows great promise in generative pre-training for convnets. The improvements on COCO tasks are more significant than those on ImageNet, indicating the representations learned by SparK are highly transferable. To summarize, SparK provides:\n\u2022 A first hierarchical masked modeling method for convnets, overcoming their inability to handle irregular masked inputs and fully exploiting their advantage of multi-scale structure.\n\u2022 A leap in convnet\u2019s performance across downstream tasks, enabling convnets to surpass self-supervised vision transformers with the similar model capacity.\n\u2022 An insight into designing generative pre-training for convnets, and a promise of extending the success of transformer\u2019s pretrain-finetune paradigm to convnets.\nThe computer vision community recently has paid more attention to research on vision transformers, while convnets appear to be no longer in the spotlight (Liu et al., 2021; 2022a; Bao et al., 2021; He et al., 2021). Nonetheless, convolutional neural networks, which condense the essence (scaleand translation-equivariant; locality; weight-sharing; hardware-friendly) of many classical vision processing systems (Lowe, 1999; Csurka et al., 2004; J\u00e9gou et al., 2010; Farabet et al., 2010), still play an irreplaceable role in tackling a wide range of real-world visual tasks that are more challenging or structural than classification (Liu et al., 2017; 2022b). We hope SparK\u2019s excellent performance compared to transformer-based pre-training will prompt us to revisit convnets as generic backbones for computer vision, and inspire more future research in exploiting their full potential."
